00:54:34,627 --> 00:54:36,688
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, how else?

806
00:54:37,820 --> 00:54:38,240
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

807
00:54:46,522 --> 00:54:50,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, so we've shown that McCarthy's a pretty good manager in spite of everything, huh?

808
00:54:51,002 --> 00:54:51,462
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

809
00:54:52,242 --> 00:55:06,425
[SPEAKER_00]: But I would think that the management would have been pretty, you know, not sharp enough

810
00:55:07,948 --> 00:55:09,008
[SPEAKER_00]: careful about him.

811
00:55:10,389 --> 00:55:14,970
[SPEAKER_00]: He was as alcoholic as we would love to believe.

812
00:55:15,010 --> 00:55:17,291
[SPEAKER_00]: I wasn't still on sleeping with that kid.

813
00:55:18,151 --> 00:55:29,615
[SPEAKER_02]: There again, as I said, I think in the tape, when a guy's got statistics like that, especially in World Series, I mean, you take those chances.

814
00:55:32,195 --> 00:55:36,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Mrs. Houston arrived late in the interview and

815
00:55:38,299 --> 00:55:41,561
[SPEAKER_00]: after this refreshments, but we were getting ready to leave.

816
00:55:41,641 --> 00:55:48,225
[SPEAKER_00]: And if we had taken them, I bet it would have dragged on another hour and a half, and we'd have eaten supper there, at least.

817
00:55:48,546 --> 00:55:54,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, about 3 o'clock, he announced that the Texas Longhorns were on them.

818
00:55:54,209 --> 00:55:55,750
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was an old Longhorn.

819
00:55:56,470 --> 00:55:57,311
[SPEAKER_00]: We got the idea.

820
00:55:57,331 --> 00:55:59,612
[SPEAKER_00]: He wanted to wrap the interview up and watch the game.

821
00:55:59,652 --> 00:56:03,175
[SPEAKER_00]: So we were already equipped, and I suggested, well, you don't know.

822
00:56:03,195 --> 00:56:03,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's go on.

823
00:56:03,995 --> 00:56:06,837
[SPEAKER_00]: So we went on another hour after that, and more.

824
00:56:07,950 --> 00:56:10,151
[SPEAKER_00]: So he was in no hurry to end the thing.

825
00:56:11,491 --> 00:56:17,474
[SPEAKER_00]: And I really haven't had too many experiences.

826
00:56:17,494 --> 00:56:25,337
[SPEAKER_00]: I've had a few where they like to want to go on and feel a little disappointed when they do leave, but he was still wound up.

827
00:56:25,437 --> 00:56:27,398
[SPEAKER_00]: He's been doing a lot of talking to, I'm sure.

828
00:56:27,438 --> 00:56:29,039
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a tiring experience for him.

829
00:56:29,799 --> 00:56:32,720
[SPEAKER_00]: When he got out of there, he didn't have his air conditioner on either.

830
00:56:32,740 --> 00:56:33,721
[SPEAKER_00]: He forgot about that.

831
00:56:33,741 --> 00:56:36,362
[SPEAKER_00]: But he was so talkative.

832
00:56:44,619 --> 00:56:49,042
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a good athlete, pretty good hitter.

833
00:56:49,302 --> 00:56:50,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Where does he live?

834
00:56:51,043 --> 00:56:51,824
[SPEAKER_01]: In Mississippi.

835
00:56:51,864 --> 00:56:54,665
[SPEAKER_01]: He's been coaching at some college there.

836
00:56:54,685 --> 00:56:56,887
[SPEAKER_01]: He stayed with the Red Sox for a while as a coach.

837
00:56:57,607 --> 00:56:59,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Worked at Louisville.

838
00:57:02,230 --> 00:57:03,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Southern Mississippi University.

839
00:57:04,031 --> 00:57:09,155
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a baseball coach and coordinator of some of this stuff.

840
00:57:09,175 --> 00:57:10,235
[SPEAKER_01]: He's been there for years.

841
00:57:15,791 --> 00:57:17,513
[SPEAKER_00]: In 47, I got your record here.

842
00:57:17,593 --> 00:57:19,014
[SPEAKER_00]: It tapered down a little bit.

843
00:57:19,034 --> 00:57:20,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Were you having problems that year?

844
00:57:20,795 --> 00:57:21,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, sir.

845
00:57:21,936 --> 00:57:23,877
[SPEAKER_00]: You were beginning to, what happened?

846
00:57:25,318 --> 00:57:26,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's another long story.

847
00:57:27,860 --> 00:57:38,148
[SPEAKER_01]: I had, this started back in, well, when I was in the service, actually, or before that.

848
00:57:41,210 --> 00:57:42,351
[SPEAKER_01]: I had a bad elbow.

849
00:57:47,782 --> 00:57:55,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Finally, I pitched too long with it, I guess, and I had a bone swerve out of it.

850
00:57:56,990 --> 00:58:05,336
[SPEAKER_01]: And in 1947, the circulation went out completely.

851
00:58:05,917 --> 00:58:12,242
[SPEAKER_01]: I was pitching against the Yankees in Fenway, and I never had anything hurt me as bad in my life.

852
00:58:13,422 --> 00:58:15,804
[SPEAKER_01]: It felt like they had disarming advice.

853
00:58:15,884 --> 00:58:18,265
[SPEAKER_01]: It felt like I had an iron ball on my ear.

854
00:58:19,386 --> 00:58:21,988
[SPEAKER_01]: I bounced the ball up to the plate and walked off.

855
00:58:23,209 --> 00:58:31,675
[SPEAKER_01]: And immediately they sent me to, within five minutes, this circulation came back.

856
00:58:31,975 --> 00:58:33,897
[SPEAKER_01]: It was limited in this right arm.

857
00:58:34,797 --> 00:58:35,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Came back.

858
00:58:35,618 --> 00:58:39,400
[SPEAKER_01]: They sent me to neurosurgeon, Dr. John Hodgson.

859
00:58:41,382 --> 00:58:42,863
[SPEAKER_01]: They thought I had a pinched nerve.

860
00:58:44,091 --> 00:58:51,714
[SPEAKER_01]: He said that there was... examined, found out there was limited circulation.

861
00:58:51,734 --> 00:58:52,815
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, you don't need me.

862
00:58:52,835 --> 00:58:53,775
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm an aerial surgeon.

863
00:58:54,375 --> 00:58:56,776
[SPEAKER_01]: I made an appointment then with Dr. E.D.

864
00:58:57,237 --> 00:59:01,518
[SPEAKER_01]: O'Neill, who was the foremost vascular surgeon.

865
00:59:03,059 --> 00:59:08,261
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm sure that they had gotten reports from Dr. Thompson.

866
00:59:08,922 --> 00:59:12,263
[SPEAKER_01]: O'Neill examined me the next day, walking down the street,

867
00:59:13,327 --> 00:59:19,351
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't even take...

868
00:59:19,411 --> 00:59:20,452
[SPEAKER_01]: I took my coat off.

869
00:59:21,693 --> 00:59:23,134
[SPEAKER_01]: He asked me to hold my hands up.

870
00:59:23,174 --> 00:59:25,515
[SPEAKER_01]: I held my hands up, brought them down.

871
00:59:26,096 --> 00:59:28,017
[SPEAKER_01]: This one's still black, this one's red.

872
00:59:29,098 --> 00:59:34,181
[SPEAKER_01]: His diagnosis was that the sympathetic nerve was in spasms.

873
00:59:34,221 --> 00:59:36,442
[SPEAKER_01]: He had contracted and wasn't relaxed.

874
00:59:37,283 --> 00:59:40,585
[SPEAKER_01]: The solution here is to cut this...

875
00:59:42,184 --> 00:59:49,870
[SPEAKER_01]: the sympathetic nerve back there and relax it.

876
00:59:51,872 --> 00:59:58,618
[SPEAKER_01]: I went back to the ballpark, reported to Joe Cronin, told him what O'Neal had said.

877
01:00:00,519 --> 01:00:01,940
[SPEAKER_01]: We needed to do this surgery.

878
01:00:03,302 --> 01:00:06,664
[SPEAKER_01]: And Joe asked me, he said, when are you going to have it done?

879
01:00:07,745 --> 01:00:11,008
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, Joe, I'm not going to have it done now.

880
01:00:11,962 --> 01:00:12,522
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, why?

881
01:00:12,602 --> 01:00:17,664
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, I'm not satisfied with Dr. O'Neill's analysis of the situation.

882
01:00:19,224 --> 01:00:23,826
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, he's one of the world's best.

883
01:00:23,866 --> 01:00:28,967
[SPEAKER_01]: They brought people royalty from England over here for Dr. O'Neill to do surgery on.

884
01:00:30,228 --> 01:00:34,149
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, I want to go back and talk to the neurosurgeon, Dr. Hudson.

885
01:00:34,709 --> 01:00:36,390
[SPEAKER_01]: I went back and I asked Dr. Hudson.

886
01:00:37,230 --> 01:00:40,111
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, if I have this type of surgery done,

887
01:00:41,075 --> 01:00:42,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Will I be able to pitch again?

888
01:00:44,236 --> 01:00:55,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Dr. Hodgson told me, he said, I've had people come to me that have had this type of surgery that couldn't play a piano or use a clap rack.

889
01:00:56,919 --> 01:00:58,819
[SPEAKER_01]: They've come to me for corrective surgery.

890
01:00:59,100 --> 01:01:05,621
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, I'm not telling you that a man of O'Neill's caliber did this surgery, but I'm just telling you what happened.

891
01:01:06,441 --> 01:01:08,802
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, well, doctor, if I have this gun,

892
01:01:09,529 --> 01:01:10,709
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to do it.

893
01:01:11,029 --> 01:01:12,910
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a miracle.

894
01:01:13,630 --> 01:01:14,930
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not going back till later.

895
01:01:17,471 --> 01:01:24,713
[SPEAKER_01]: So I sat around Boston for six, seven weeks.

896
01:01:27,833 --> 01:01:34,855
[SPEAKER_01]: They had people, doctors, who'd read about this, that would take, have a day off, come to the ballpark.

897
01:01:35,955 --> 01:01:37,356
[SPEAKER_01]: And they came in to the clubhouse.

898
01:01:37,396 --> 01:01:38,336
[SPEAKER_01]: I'd sit around doing it.

899
01:01:39,920 --> 01:01:42,443
[SPEAKER_01]: And they discussed my case.

900
01:01:43,203 --> 01:01:47,928
[SPEAKER_01]: They had me on a drug to stimulate circulation.

901
01:01:47,948 --> 01:01:50,330
[SPEAKER_01]: They did everything, run tests.

902
01:01:52,092 --> 01:02:03,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And one doctor, a young doctor, an American border surgery man, told me, he said, well, I can determine whether it's a sympathetic nerve infection.

903
01:02:08,682 --> 01:02:10,964
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, we'll go to the hospital.

904
01:02:10,984 --> 01:02:12,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Made a formal regret at the hospital.

905
01:02:14,206 --> 01:02:22,631
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, I can inject Novacaine into the ganglia of that nerve and deaden it, which will have the same effect as cutting.

906
01:02:23,232 --> 01:02:24,072
[SPEAKER_01]: It will relax it.

907
01:02:26,814 --> 01:02:31,938
[SPEAKER_01]: They put me face down on that table that's only on that side of the outside corner.

908
01:02:32,578 --> 01:02:37,001
[SPEAKER_01]: But he's got to get with his big needle, file needle, he's got to get into it.

909
01:02:37,515 --> 01:02:42,777
[SPEAKER_01]: right under the third thoracic vertebra to get into the ganglion of that nerve.

910
01:02:43,577 --> 01:02:45,157
[SPEAKER_01]: And I could feel that thing go off.

911
01:02:45,177 --> 01:02:47,058
[SPEAKER_01]: I squeezed the sawdust out of that table.

912
01:02:48,118 --> 01:02:55,020
[SPEAKER_01]: And when he hit the bone here, well, he finally, anyway, he had a nurse here all the time, had her there checking him for him.

913
01:02:55,620 --> 01:02:56,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing happened.

914
01:02:58,221 --> 01:03:04,163
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, well, maybe, maybe that we didn't get

915
01:03:04,732 --> 01:03:06,373
[SPEAKER_01]: an efficient or effective block.

916
01:03:06,533 --> 01:03:09,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's come back the day after tomorrow and we'll do it all over again.

917
01:03:09,774 --> 01:03:11,855
[SPEAKER_01]: We went back and did the same thing again.

918
01:03:12,515 --> 01:03:20,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Got so much snow that came in my bloodstream that I felt like I was floating up drifting, you know.

919
01:03:21,739 --> 01:03:26,361
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, I'm satisfied that we got an effective block.

920
01:03:26,461 --> 01:03:28,081
[SPEAKER_01]: It is not the sympathetic nerve.

921
01:03:28,142 --> 01:03:28,882
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the spasm.

922
01:03:29,642 --> 01:03:34,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what I wondered, why didn't a man of O'Neill's caliber think of something like this, you know?

923
01:03:35,588 --> 01:03:36,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyway, I came home.

924
01:03:36,648 --> 01:03:39,289
[SPEAKER_01]: I was supposed to go to Dr. Albert Ochsner in New Orleans.

925
01:03:40,890 --> 01:03:46,631
[SPEAKER_01]: On the way home, Dr. Albert Ochsner died, the foremost fascist man in the country.

926
01:03:46,731 --> 01:03:50,912
[SPEAKER_01]: I made about 20 trips to San Antonio, back and forth.

927
01:03:50,932 --> 01:03:52,613
[SPEAKER_01]: They finally diagnosed the trouble.

928
01:03:54,093 --> 01:03:55,194
[SPEAKER_01]: They'd had the x-rays.

929
01:03:55,214 --> 01:03:59,275
[SPEAKER_01]: They'd done everything they could all the time I was in Boston that lasted six weeks.

930
01:04:04,827 --> 01:04:12,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Injected Novacaine into the spleen and side of his muscle up here and got a case of blood pressure.

931
01:04:12,373 --> 01:04:13,373
[SPEAKER_01]: We went through all this.

932
01:04:14,454 --> 01:04:18,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyway, they cut a segment out of the muscle in my leg up here.

933
01:04:19,218 --> 01:04:20,338
[SPEAKER_01]: This is in 1947.

934
01:04:20,879 --> 01:04:25,642
[SPEAKER_01]: I get home Thanksgiving Day.

935
01:04:26,003 --> 01:04:26,563
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm over there.

936
01:04:27,464 --> 01:04:33,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I went to, back to Johns Hopkins where I was supposed to be.

937
01:04:33,912 --> 01:04:39,455
[SPEAKER_01]: at the end of the season, but I didn't make it to the end, to have this elbow operated on.

938
01:04:39,975 --> 01:04:41,075
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's George Bennett.

939
01:04:41,936 --> 01:04:49,119
[SPEAKER_01]: I went there and had this elbow operated on in 1947, and I got home Christmas Eve in May.

940
01:04:49,759 --> 01:04:53,701
[SPEAKER_01]: But Dr. Bennett would not operate on it for two reasons.

941
01:04:53,721 --> 01:04:58,103
[SPEAKER_01]: The first thing, I don't need the operation if I'm not going to fix it in the end.

942
01:04:58,463 --> 01:05:02,025
[SPEAKER_01]: But the main reason is, with the limited circulation, it wouldn't heal.

943
01:05:03,660 --> 01:05:05,261
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, not enough blood circulation.

944
01:05:05,401 --> 01:05:06,322
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't heal properly.

945
01:05:07,903 --> 01:05:13,046
[SPEAKER_01]: But after I got the blood circulation restored to a degree, then I went back.

946
01:05:19,050 --> 01:05:21,072
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how was it the next season then?

947
01:05:23,313 --> 01:05:24,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I went back.

948
01:05:25,034 --> 01:05:31,138
[SPEAKER_01]: They told me then, this was done late in December, that I wouldn't be able to fish 100% until June.

949
01:05:33,110 --> 01:05:45,195
[SPEAKER_01]: I went back in 1948, and I had to throw.

950
01:05:47,256 --> 01:05:48,077
[SPEAKER_01]: It was swell up.

951
01:05:49,497 --> 01:05:51,598
[SPEAKER_01]: I was having trouble.

952
01:05:53,399 --> 01:06:00,162
[SPEAKER_01]: May the 15th, cutting down time to the 25-foot limit, they optioned me to move.

953
01:06:01,305 --> 01:06:02,846
[SPEAKER_01]: They were going out to make the Louisville.

954
01:06:02,886 --> 01:06:07,568
[SPEAKER_01]: I told Joe Cronin, I said, McCarthy's managing the Red Sox at this time.

955
01:06:08,228 --> 01:06:10,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Cronin had moved up as president, mostly.

956
01:06:12,029 --> 01:06:14,490
[SPEAKER_01]: And I told Joe, I said, I'm not going to Louisville.

957
01:06:15,591 --> 01:06:20,573
[SPEAKER_01]: McCarthy had the total say as to who he kept and who the players or trades and what have you.

958
01:06:22,033 --> 01:06:25,775
[SPEAKER_01]: And I told Joe, I said, I'll tell you what I'll do.

959
01:06:25,815 --> 01:06:27,075
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll go back to Austin.

960
01:06:28,416 --> 01:06:30,737
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll make my own arrangements with my staff tonight.

961
01:06:33,944 --> 01:06:36,925
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, I live 30 miles from Austin.

962
01:06:37,445 --> 01:06:41,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Then if I'm through, my family and wife and kids will be at home.

963
01:06:42,888 --> 01:06:46,409
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said, if I could pitch at Louisville, I could pitch here.

964
01:06:50,011 --> 01:06:51,451
[SPEAKER_01]: So I came back to Austin.

965
01:06:51,491 --> 01:06:57,894
[SPEAKER_01]: I played, stayed in Austin from May the 15th through July.

966
01:06:58,494 --> 01:07:00,595
[SPEAKER_01]: I finally pitched nine in the ballgame in Austin.

967
01:07:01,795 --> 01:07:13,598
[SPEAKER_01]: And I called Joe Cronin and, around the office I was at, and Joe just asked me, he said, Tex, I want to ask you one question.

968
01:07:13,638 --> 01:07:15,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you help this ball club?

969
01:07:15,778 --> 01:07:22,060
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, Joe, I can help your ball club if I'm used to it.

970
01:07:23,760 --> 01:07:30,902
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, without even putting the phone down, he said, well, make your own arrangements, and we'll reimburse you when you get it.

971
01:07:30,922 --> 01:07:31,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Come on back.

972
01:07:32,130 --> 01:07:36,732
[SPEAKER_01]: When I got back, McCarthy didn't hardly recognize me at all.

973
01:07:38,452 --> 01:07:44,294
[SPEAKER_01]: I pitched seven and one-third innings in about three or four different balls.

974
01:07:45,015 --> 01:07:45,975
[SPEAKER_01]: I gave up one hit.

975
01:07:47,376 --> 01:07:53,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Dale Mitchell, Cleveland, got a base hit off of me between short and third on a ground ball.

976
01:07:54,138 --> 01:07:55,198
[SPEAKER_01]: The only hit I gave up.

977
01:07:55,879 --> 01:07:59,600
[SPEAKER_01]: They had me throwing every day in one game in particular.

978
01:08:00,978 --> 01:08:03,680
[SPEAKER_01]: I was up in the first inning, didn't get the ball game.

979
01:08:04,661 --> 01:08:06,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Again in the third, fifth.

980
01:08:06,543 --> 01:08:11,607
[SPEAKER_01]: I told Larry Woodall, I said, Larry, I said, I can't be used like this.

981
01:08:13,709 --> 01:08:19,133
[SPEAKER_01]: I could pitch five innings, six innings, or two innings.

982
01:08:19,834 --> 01:08:22,536
[SPEAKER_01]: But this thing was sitting up swell up and getting fluid up.

983
01:08:23,297 --> 01:08:26,739
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, I was trying to help.

984
01:08:27,220 --> 01:08:29,582
[SPEAKER_01]: I told Larry, I said, I will not be able

985
01:08:30,753 --> 01:08:35,637
[SPEAKER_01]: to do justice to myself or the ball club, either one.

986
01:08:35,977 --> 01:08:41,341
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, I warmed up four or five times today, I'll be in the bullpen again tomorrow.

987
01:08:42,022 --> 01:08:48,467
[SPEAKER_01]: I've got to have some power in the play, regardless of whether I throw in the bullpen or the ball game or what.

988
01:08:49,147 --> 01:08:51,890
[SPEAKER_01]: I wasn't joking, I just wanted to play.

989
01:08:53,191 --> 01:08:58,775
[SPEAKER_01]: And I told Larry that, he told McCarthy, McCarthy

990
01:09:01,328 --> 01:09:03,109
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, well, him.

991
01:09:04,190 --> 01:09:11,757
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, black him, he's not going to help us anyway.

992
01:09:16,180 --> 01:09:20,544
[SPEAKER_00]: So in 49, did you stay with the clubhouse the rest of the season?

993
01:09:20,604 --> 01:09:21,424
[SPEAKER_01]: I stayed through 48.

994
01:09:21,585 --> 01:09:23,226
[SPEAKER_01]: 1949, McCarthy's still there.

995
01:09:23,246 --> 01:09:24,067
[SPEAKER_01]: In 49, I was 100%.

996
01:09:32,636 --> 01:09:40,261
[SPEAKER_01]: And down in Florida, I pitched good balls.

997
