1497
01:43:36,021 --> 01:43:38,843
[SPEAKER_01]: He was going to run up and back like a wonder curtain.

1498
01:43:38,863 --> 01:43:39,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Fell on the ball.

1499
01:43:41,307 --> 01:43:42,848
[SPEAKER_01]: That's one way they retaliate.

1500
01:43:44,489 --> 01:43:45,529
[SPEAKER_01]: They can, you know.

1501
01:43:46,149 --> 01:43:48,511
[SPEAKER_01]: But he was going to drag bump me.

1502
01:43:48,791 --> 01:43:49,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Make me cover the face.

1503
01:43:50,772 --> 01:43:51,452
[SPEAKER_02]: Run over me.

1504
01:43:52,733 --> 01:43:53,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Bump into me.

1505
01:43:55,314 --> 01:43:56,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Crack that off.

1506
01:43:56,655 --> 01:43:57,295
[SPEAKER_01]: What a way.

1507
01:43:59,756 --> 01:44:01,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what's the next section?

1508
01:44:02,038 --> 01:44:02,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Spitballs.

1509
01:44:03,078 --> 01:44:03,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Spitballs.

1510
01:44:04,279 --> 01:44:05,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Emery balls.

1511
01:44:05,459 --> 01:44:06,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Grease balls.

1512
01:44:07,220 --> 01:44:09,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I can tell you stories about that if you've got time.

1513
01:44:10,022 --> 01:44:10,562
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

1514
01:44:11,852 --> 01:44:19,054
[SPEAKER_01]: By the way, the Texas Longhorns are playing Arkansas in the College World Series starting right now.

1515
01:44:19,774 --> 01:44:22,274
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I play the Texas Longhorns.

1516
01:44:23,215 --> 01:44:26,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Roger Clements and all those boys, Texas Longhorns.

1517
01:44:27,716 --> 01:44:31,597
[SPEAKER_01]: I can pick it up later and read the papers.

1518
01:44:40,972 --> 01:44:50,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Mike Raver was an old catcher, relief pitcher, and I started throwing spitballs at him once in a while.

1519
01:44:51,117 --> 01:44:54,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Mike told me, he said, you've got the best spitter of anybody I've ever seen.

1520
01:44:57,040 --> 01:45:06,585
[SPEAKER_01]: And we had two pitchers on our football team, Dick Newsome in the early 40s and Charlie Wagner.

1521
01:45:06,925 --> 01:45:07,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Charlie Wagner.

1522
01:45:10,018 --> 01:45:11,018
[SPEAKER_01]: They both told me that.

1523
01:45:11,899 --> 01:45:19,921
[SPEAKER_01]: I told that nurse of mine, I said, you old North Carolina hillbilly, I said, you're not coming back, you prude.

1524
01:45:20,881 --> 01:45:24,722
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, I'll give you a thousand dollars to tell me how you know.

1525
01:45:25,302 --> 01:45:27,743
[SPEAKER_01]: He denied it to his mother or anybody, you know.

1526
01:45:28,323 --> 01:45:28,803
[SPEAKER_01]: I knew.

1527
01:45:29,804 --> 01:45:30,864
[SPEAKER_01]: But, anyway.

1528
01:45:31,024 --> 01:45:31,944
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that old Bobo?

1529
01:45:32,664 --> 01:45:32,904
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1530
01:45:33,064 --> 01:45:34,085
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, not Bobo.

1531
01:45:34,285 --> 01:45:35,085
[SPEAKER_01]: This is Heber.

1532
01:45:35,947 --> 01:45:39,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Bobo was pictured for St. Louis in Detroit.

1533
01:45:39,129 --> 01:45:42,071
[SPEAKER_01]: He was, but one day I threw a stick, once a day probably.

1534
01:45:42,471 --> 01:45:48,334
[SPEAKER_01]: I never threw it unless I had two stacks, or I never threw it unless I was in a big jam.

1535
01:46:06,271 --> 01:46:11,772
[SPEAKER_01]: And there was no use of throwing it because 90% of the time the batter is going to miss it.

1536
01:46:13,052 --> 01:46:22,234
[SPEAKER_01]: I would just with these two fingers, I learned to work with it and let the ball go just like Scott throws the court ball now.

1537
01:46:22,535 --> 01:46:27,316
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I'd split the finger with this.

1538
01:46:29,136 --> 01:46:33,457
[SPEAKER_01]: One day in St. Louis, George McQuinn, the first baseman,

1539
01:46:34,723 --> 01:46:37,905
[SPEAKER_01]: men on base, I stuck him out with a stick.

1540
01:46:39,085 --> 01:46:45,869
[SPEAKER_01]: A few days later, we were on the outpost, and I walked into the hotel.

1541
01:46:54,714 --> 01:46:56,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe I'm warming up too much.

1542
01:46:56,635 --> 01:47:01,398
[SPEAKER_01]: I stuck George McCrim, rushed across the lobby,

1543
01:47:02,426 --> 01:47:04,788
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, Ted, you're cheating, son of a bitch.

1544
01:47:05,768 --> 01:47:07,169
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, what's the matter with you, George?

1545
01:47:07,750 --> 01:47:09,711
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, you know you struck me out the other day.

1546
01:47:10,152 --> 01:47:11,973
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, I got halfway back to the bench.

1547
01:47:11,993 --> 01:47:13,034
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, I jumped that high.

1548
01:47:13,054 --> 01:47:16,436
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, I thought about that dirty son of a bitch doing that to me.

1549
01:47:16,476 --> 01:47:18,898
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, George, you know that didn't happen.

1550
01:47:18,918 --> 01:47:20,639
[SPEAKER_01]: You know I wouldn't do anything like that.

1551
01:47:21,600 --> 01:47:29,205
[SPEAKER_01]: But there was one day with Fannie Yankees in Boston.

1552
01:47:32,407 --> 01:47:34,147
[SPEAKER_01]: I heard a couple of men on, two men out.

1553
01:47:35,247 --> 01:47:37,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Joe DiMaggio up front, two strikes.

1554
01:47:39,728 --> 01:47:43,149
[SPEAKER_00]: I struck him out with a spitter.

1555
01:47:44,069 --> 01:47:46,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Joe just swung right through.

1556
01:47:47,890 --> 01:47:48,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Side's retired.

1557
01:47:49,290 --> 01:47:50,550
[SPEAKER_01]: His head's caved up a bit.

1558
01:47:52,170 --> 01:47:58,692
[SPEAKER_01]: I know he said something to the third base coach about it later, to watch it.

1559
01:48:04,910 --> 01:48:09,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Later in the ball game, Tommy Hennig was a cop.

1560
01:48:10,454 --> 01:48:14,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Johnny Peacock, he knew when I had one loaded.

1561
01:48:14,818 --> 01:48:19,221
[SPEAKER_01]: If you'd watch him, you could tell nearly when I had one loaded.

1562
01:48:19,261 --> 01:48:22,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Peacock would get the cops in time to catch it.

1563
01:48:22,884 --> 01:48:29,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the ball hit Peacock here on the end of the glove and rolled off across the red dirt here.

1564
01:48:29,870 --> 01:48:33,893
[SPEAKER_01]: And Art Fletcher, coach at the College of Film and Art, take a look at that ball, you know.

1565
01:48:34,553 --> 01:48:35,954
[SPEAKER_01]: except to look at it a little.

1566
01:48:37,054 --> 01:48:41,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Right before the spot was on the ball, a red play picked it up, you know.

1567
01:48:42,597 --> 01:48:48,860
[SPEAKER_01]: And after the game, Bill Summert for some time, old Sea Pig he was called in short there.

1568
01:48:48,940 --> 01:48:49,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Sea Pig?

1569
01:48:49,941 --> 01:48:52,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Sea Pig was his nickname, Summert.

1570
01:48:52,962 --> 01:48:55,964
[SPEAKER_01]: And he came to me after the ball game.

1571
01:48:57,625 --> 01:49:02,307
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, Tex, god damn it, I want you to quit throwing that thing.

1572
01:49:02,765 --> 01:49:07,389
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, you got good stuff, you don't need it, and I'm going to retire in a year or two.

1573
01:49:07,849 --> 01:49:15,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Then after I retire, you can throw it all you want to, but don't embarrass me anymore.

1574
01:49:16,237 --> 01:49:18,338
[SPEAKER_02]: Did he teach you how to do it?

